From eric@goonsquad.spies.com Wed Oct  2 22:23:08 1996
Message-ID: <m0v8dQx-001UuhC@goonsquad.spies.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Oct 1996 19:22:43 -0700 (PDT)
From: Eric Smith <eric@goonsquad.spies.com>
Subject: Re: UDB Install Problems...

Roel Lascano <rlascano@mystech.com> wrote about putting new memory in a UDB:
> been seated and reseated several times.  NT4.0 is *much* happier, but
> I'm still getting:
> 
> lca: machine check (la=0xfffffc000208490, pc=0xfffffc0000251aa0)
>  Reason: correctable ECC error (short frame, retryable, dc_stat=0);
>          Correctable read error to b-cache occurred at 251ab0
>          Several other correctable errors occurred.

Assuming you have real 36-bit SIMMs, not 32-bit or some crappy "logic parity"
SIMMs, if you get lots of these as you describe it means some of your memory
is bad.  From the address you could determine which pair of SIMMs it is, if
you knew how (which I don't).  The easiest thing to do is to try running Linux
with only one pair at a time.  There are only six combinations of four items
taken two at a time, so it shouldn't take you too terribly long to track down
which is bad.

There is a also a somewhat less likely explanation:  one of your SIMM sockets 
may have a bad contact or a bad solder joint.

Cheers,
Eric

