From eric@goonsquad.spies.com Tue Oct 22 15:12:41 1996
Message-ID: <m0vFmCc-001UwvC@goonsquad.spies.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Oct 1996 12:09:26 -0700 (PDT)
From: Eric Smith <eric@goonsquad.spies.com>
Subject: Re: UDB Questions

"Shane Anderson" <shane@nve.com>
> >"Shane Anderson" said:
> > blah blah...  SCSI termination on the UDB?  blah blah...
> 
> >then Jay said:
> > (paraphrased)  No termination inside the UDB... *MUST* terminate 
> > externally
> 
> >then Maurice said:
> > As a matter of fact, I found that placing an in-line terminator right at the
> > port, then another at the far end of the SCSI bus made it much happier if
> > you hang 4 external devices on the UDB (like I did).
> 
> Hmm.. This rather scares me.  And I've noticed some problems when I 
> hook up my ZIP drive (using a 6' cable...)  And it is terminated, but 
> maybe the problem is missing the terminator on the other end.  It 
> seems to me that there ought to have been a terminator at the 
> controller that is always on... (much like Maurices in-line 
> terminator...)

If Jay said there was no termination inside the UDB, he was mistaken.  But
I think what he actually said was that the 2.5 inch *drive* had no
termination.

The UDB has exactly one internal SCSI terminator soldered on the PCI riser.
It can't be disabled.  If you added two external terminators you bus is
over-terminated, which can cause problems and possibly damage the line
drivers of SCSI chips.  You should only add one external terminator at the
very end of the SCSI bus.

The internal disk in the UDB is not terminated, and can't be.  Digital
claims that they can get away with this due to the short stub length.  When
I was having problems I looked at some of the SCSI signals on an
oscilliscope and they didn't look too bad.

Cheers,
Eric

