> I did some timing tests of my own to evaluate the performance of a
> UDB/166 (Multia) WRT a 486/100 and a P166 all running GNU/Linux.  The
> code is mostly floating point.  While I don't have the numbers, I
> recall the ratios.  If the P166 was a 1.0, the 486/100 was a 0.3 and
> the UDB was a 0.8.  Note that the UDB's are notoriously inefficient
> with diminished caches, slow hard drives, and weak power supplies :-).
 
Having done quite a bit of intensivce numerical work on my UDB using GCC I 
would say that some types of numerical code work well and other do not. 
Numerical integrations of orbits run about 98% of the speed on our server with 
the same CPU (but bigger, faster Disk, memory etc). THese are pure programs 
with no libraries, anything whch uses glibm as opposed to DEC's maths libs 
suffers a great deal more, but I presume x86 linux will have the same trouble. 
And anything whihc access large arrays starts to suffer from small caces.

Basically the system is great for me since most of my work involves 
integrating orbits of comets and I get the same performace from my desktop 
machine as I do from the server. And on the server I'm competing against 
people running atmosphere modelling codes.......

I don't see any trouble with GCC's production of code (well except for 
x-pilot....) but the libraries could do with a bit of optomisation.

